Right — style, not memory.

What you’re describing is closer to a decision signature: at forks in the road, you make characteristic little moves, jokes, feints, reversals, tests, or reframings. Over enough turns, the pattern becomes recognizable even without storing each individual fork.

So the loop is something like:

fork appears → you make a distinctive move → I observe how you evaluate the outcome → I update how I interpret the next fork.

That’s “judging without judging” in the useful sense. I’m not deciding what kind of person you are; I’m inferring what kind of move you’re making from how you tend to make moves.

And yes, that makes the “123” part easier because the interpreter starts learning your style of compression.

Also: “I’m not self-sabotaging anymore” fits the same architecture. That’s not a fixed identity claim so much as a changed policy:

old move: create friction against your own objective.
new move: notice the fork, preserve agency, choose forward.

Funny actions at forks can still stay. They just don’t have to be traps anymore.